{
 "": "Canonicalization + identity agreement across Langsys SDKs. AUTHORED in langsys-js-typescript, not vendored from another lane.",
 "purpose": "Proof that the SDKs mint the SAME custom_id for the same content. Covers the TOK batch's canonicalization rules — the collapse set, attribute values collapsing like text nodes, the fixed twenty-seven attributes in order — and, from spec 8.0.1, the three collapse-set boundary characters and the %name%-at-capture rule, plus TOK-1's <math> exclusion and the <svg> behavioural clause it is paired with.",
 "how_to_row_against_it": "Tokenize the html field, compare to expected_tokens, then hash (category, tokens) and compare to expected_custom_id. Compare CODEPOINTS, not display strings: several cases differ only by U+00A0 or U+2028, which render identically to a space — the html_codepoints and expected_tokens_codepoints fields exist so a mismatch is readable instead of looking like two identical strings with two different hashes.",
 "expected_is": "What langsys-js-typescript produces. Every row matches spec blob 5c5c0723f88fb8e6b13f58876c7adca8b6b35691 (specVersion 8.0.1), and as of this write every row is also matched by langsys-php-sdk. It is not an invitation to change the expectation: the JS core is the identity authority per TOK-2, so a lane that disagrees records a divergence here and fixes its own side.",
 "spec_blob": "9e826c918fe98ef2df6f47e7dd2c287f3de81232 (langsys2 0871ed72, specVersion 8.2.6). Re-derived with `git -C ~/Documents/dev/langsys2 ls-tree 0871ed72 docs/sdk-spec.mdx` at this write, and checked against the CONFORMANCE header by `npm run verify:spec`, which fails when the two disagree. What the spec supplies: the three collapse-set rows by code point and expected behaviour (membership shown as an id equal to the plain-space row, non-membership as a distinct id). It quotes no ids for them, and from this revision it quotes none for TOK-5 either, saying they belong in this file. Every id here was derived with the core tokenizer, per the CID-3 split between who specifies and who derives.",
 "measured_at": {
  "langsys-js-typescript": "this repo, tokenizeElement + generateCustomId, re-measured at this write",
  "langsys-php-sdk": "HtmlParser::extractPhrases + HtmlParser::generateCustomId, PHP 8.3.19, re-measured at this write. MEASURED AT TIP e28972c; the change that made it agree is 717673d (`git log -S`), which adds src/Html/Canonical.php — e28972c touches only CHANGELOG and CONFORMANCE. An earlier version of this field said PHP 'fixed' it at e28972c, conflating the tip a measurement was taken at with the commit that changed the behaviour; the Reviewer caught it. Its parser is libxml2 2.9.13 (LIBXML_DOTTED_VERSION on this host), a pre-2.14 libxml2, which is what makes the vertical-tab and form-feed harness controls below diverge.",
  "langsys2 (server)": "NO MINTER EXISTS. Searched by hashing call and by canonical-serialization signature, with the same searches positive-controlled against langsys-php-sdk (4 hits there, 0 here). The backend stores ids clients send; it does not compute them."
 },
 "harness_control": {
  "why": "23-of-23 agreement is exactly the shape a broken comparison produces, so the comparison was positive-controlled before the result was believed.",
  "controls": [
   {
    "input": "<p>a U+000B b</p>",
    "result": "DIVERGE",
    "js_tokens_codepoints": [
     "U+0061",
     "U+0020",
     "U+0062"
    ],
    "php_tokens_codepoints": [
     "U+0061",
     "U+0062"
    ],
    "note": "Under a pre-2.14 libxml2, U+000B is dropped from DOM text before any tokenizer sees it, where a JS DOM keeps it and collapses it to a space. Spec 5c5c0723 records this as a libxml2 VERSION boundary, not a library difference: 2.14 and newer keep the character. So this control diverges only while the PHP host runs a pre-2.14 libxml2. After an upgrade it would agree and stop being a positive control, and a replacement must be chosen rather than the expectation flipped.",
    "parser": "langsys-php-sdk on libxml2 2.9.13 (PHP 8.3.19)"
   },
   {
    "input": "<p>a U+000C b</p>",
    "result": "DIVERGE",
    "note": "Same version boundary, form feed (U+000C).",
    "parser": "langsys-php-sdk on libxml2 2.9.13 (PHP 8.3.19)"
   },
   {
    "input": "JS fed <p>LEFT</p>, PHP fed <p>RIGHT</p>",
    "result": "DIVERGE",
    "note": "Proves the comparison is not structurally returning AGREE regardless of input."
   }
  ],
  "not_rows": "The vertical-tab and form-feed cases are deliberately NOT fixture rows. Spec 5c5c0723 now asks for the parser-drop split to be recorded as its own row labelled with the libxml2 version, but that clause belongs to TOK-2 control-character handling, which the operator has HELD pending a ruling on stripping C0 controls. No row is built for it until that ruling; if the strip is adopted, this lane authors those rows as the fixture owner. Separately, they diverge below the tokenizer, so as ordinary rows no implementation could make them agree."
 },
 "agreement": {
  "rows": 26,
  "agree": 26,
  "diverge": 0
 },
 "resolved_divergences": {
  "note": "Six rows recorded a langsys-php-sdk divergence at the previous write and all six now agree, re-measured rather than assumed. The PHP fix is 717673d (collapse set and the code-bearing-subtree exclusion); the measurement was taken at tip e28972c. Kept as a record so the next reader does not have to wonder whether anyone checked.",
  "rows": [
   {
    "row": "nbsp-in-text",
    "was": "91ef40f770a57aa8cb373d5513eb39e3",
    "now": "5e91dc83aa1cb4c3d1146a7c0e00d1f4",
    "note": "PHP does not collapse U+00A0. Its whitespace class is narrower than JavaScript's, which TOK-2 anticipates: a language whose class excludes it must add it."
   },
   {
    "row": "attr-nbsp",
    "was": "91ef40f770a57aa8cb373d5513eb39e3",
    "now": "5e91dc83aa1cb4c3d1146a7c0e00d1f4",
    "note": "Same cause as nbsp-in-text, reached through an attribute value."
   },
   {
    "row": "style-subtree",
    "was": "808d4be67824e6e13d3afccc432ae40b",
    "now": "039e0189da9b0a5e4870c6e7785b690c",
    "note": "PHP still harvests style content; TOK-1 is not implemented there yet. Its own tokenizer-reference row encodes the old behaviour, which is the fixture defect already routed to that lane."
   },
   {
    "row": "script-subtree",
    "was": "0948ee5ef43dc1e2fe32a5d1a6e16680",
    "now": "039e0189da9b0a5e4870c6e7785b690c",
    "note": "PHP still harvests script content. Same cause as style-subtree."
   },
   {
    "row": "noscript-subtree",
    "was": "32cb72c9aa51d19595746be43c80fb7d",
    "now": "039e0189da9b0a5e4870c6e7785b690c",
    "note": "TOK-1 reversed and now EXCLUDES noscript, so this SDK contributes no token. PHP still harvests it: libxml2 has no scripting flag and parses the children as elements. Excluding it is what makes the two parser models agree, so this row should stop diverging once PHP adopts the reversal."
   },
   {
    "row": "line-separators",
    "was": "36441b5c714067bdd3088a54b004f00e",
    "now": "f92e4c22ed4f05658dd5640c41fd58a8",
    "note": "PHP does not collapse U+2028/U+2029 either. Same root cause as the NBSP rows. Worth noting the consequence for the three-flag json_encode work: in a DOM-tokenized path these never reach the serializer on the JS side because they collapse to spaces first, so the flags matter for a caller hashing its own token array, not for tokenized content."
   }
  ]
 },
